/* The handoff's animation set as a class vocabulary. bible 10.6 makes the
 * handoff's named animations authoritative alongside colour and spacing.
 *
 * THE CTA PRESS IS NOT HERE. It is in Theme.uss, it is correct, and bible
 * 10.6 names it specifically. Re-implementing it would be a second source.
 * What changed with this file is only that its 60ms is now
 * var(--motion-press) rather than a literal, so all three motion tokens are
 * read by something.
 *
 * WHAT IS CHECKED HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT. The plan's text for this file said
 * flatly "THIS FILE HAS NO TEST". That was the right instinct and the wrong
 * amount - some of it is checkable and was checked, and the part that is not
 * is worth naming precisely, because a vague disclaimer is how a thing ends
 * up neither tested nor looked at.
 *
 * CHECKED:
 *  - this sheet compiles to a non-zero rule count, so it is not silently
 *    inert (check-stylesheets.sh - the guard Tokens.uss earned the hard way
 *    by dying to one stray comment terminator);
 *  - every var() below names a token that exists in Tokens.uss
 *    (verify-uss-tokens.sh check 3);
 *  - the rules BIND and the durations RESOLVE. Measured once, by building
 *    one probe element per class under a real runtime panel with the shell
 *    stylesheets attached and reading resolvedStyle.transitionProperty,
 *    .transitionDuration and .transitionTimingFunction back out. The
 *    readings are in the commit message that added this file. The probe was
 *    a throwaway and was deleted - it asserted nothing a standing test could
 *    keep asserting, since the thing it proves is that these eight rules
 *    parse and match, which check-stylesheets.sh already fails on if they
 *    stop parsing - and it counts them: 8 rules out of this file, checked
 *    against the eight selectors below;
 *  - that adding this sheet changes NO RESTING FRAME. All fourteen captures
 *    in implementation/results/screens re-rendered byte-identical with it
 *    in the cascade, which is the whole of what a still can say about a
 *    transition, and is worth having as a regression guard.
 *
 * NOT CHECKED, and not checkable from this repo:
 *  - that anything ever MOVES. A transition needs a panel that ticks, and
 *    Broodline.UI.Tests has no panel. The screenshot corpus cannot stand in:
 *    a capture is ONE FRAME, and one frame of a 220ms transition is
 *    pixel-identical to no transition at all. A FIXTURE FOR THIS FILE WAS
 *    DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED - Tasks 3 and 4 each earned one because their
 *    output was invisible to the corpus, and copying that here would have
 *    produced a picture that looked like verification and verified nothing.
 *  - that the durations FEEL right. 60/120/220ms are the handoff's numbers,
 *    not measurements. Whether a push at 220ms reads brisk or sluggish under
 *    a thumb is Task 16's eyes-on pass over the running app, and motion is
 *    the one part of this phase where that pass has no substitute.
 *
 * WHAT THIS FILE NO LONGER CONTAINS, AND WHY.
 *
 * It used to carry six classes, four of which matched nothing, on the stated
 * expectation that "Tasks 7-12 are what apply this vocabulary". Those tasks
 * landed and did not. Measured at the end of the phase: motion-screen-in,
 * motion-screen-in--enter, sheet-layer--hidden and reveal-flare--hidden had
 * zero references anywhere outside this file, and ScreenHost touches none of
 * them - so the screen push, the screen pop and the sheet slide were never
 * animated by anything. reveal-flare was applied, in SpliceRevealView.uxml,
 * but nothing ever added or removed its --hidden pair, so there was no state
 * change for its transition to run against and it animated nothing either.
 *
 * A transition whose end state no code applies is not deferred work, it is a
 * claim the file cannot back - and it reads to the next author as though the
 * app has motion it does not have. So the inert rules are gone rather than
 * relabelled. What remains is what a class change actually drives today.
 * Restoring the screen and sheet transitions is a matter of landing them
 * WITH the ScreenHost code that toggles the classes, in one change, so the
 * two cannot be believed separately again.
 *
 * REVEAL-FLARE IS THE EXCEPTION AND IT WAS KEPT, because it is the only one
 * of the four that already had both a consumer and a test: the class is
 * applied to the reveal's hero card, and FirstHourScreensTests pins it there
 * precisely because "a rule that goes stale silently". What it lacked was the
 * toggle, so that was landed with it rather than deleting a payoff the rest
 * of the screen is built around. SpliceRevealView.Bind now sets the --hidden
 * state and clears it on the next frame, which is the state change the
 * transition runs against.
 *
 * VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED, for what is left: option-row is toggled by
 * OptionRow.Selected and by its own :active rule; t-num is a live class in
 * Theme.uss; reveal-flare--hidden is added and removed by SpliceRevealView.
 * Those three bite.
 *
 * ================================================================
 * NINE OF THE HANDOFF'S TEN NAMED ANIMATIONS ARE NOT HERE AND CANNOT BE,
 * AND THIS BLOCK IS SO THAT IS A RECORDED ABSENCE RATHER THAN A SILENT ONE.
 * Added by Phase 9 Task 14c, which was the first task to draw something the
 * handoff plainly spins.
 *
 * README.md's "Named animations" table lists ten, and eight of them are
 * `infinite`: glow, dash, bob, orbit, ripple, tapPing, quake, sweep. USS on
 * 6000.6.0f1 has `transition-*` and NO `@keyframes` and NO `animation`
 * property at all - a transition runs once between two states a class change
 * moves you across, which is not what any of the eight is. There is no
 * property for them to land in, so they are the same class of absence as
 * `align-items: baseline`, `text-transform` and `calc()`: things that look
 * authorable and are not. The ninth, `riseIn`, is finite and IS expressible;
 * it is simply not applied anywhere yet. The tenth, the CTA press, is in
 * Theme.uss.
 *
 * WHAT WOULD BE NEEDED, so the cost is on the record rather than rediscovered
 * by whoever wants one: a C# `schedule.Execute(...).Every(n)` writing
 * `style.rotate` / `style.opacity` / `style.translate` per frame. That is a
 * real mechanism and it works. It was declined for `orbit` in Task 14c on two
 * grounds. The first is that it spends a layout-or-repaint per frame,
 * forever, on decoration, on a mobile target. The second matters more here:
 * the screenshot corpus is the only evidence this project has that anything
 * renders, and a fixture that animates itself lands on a different frame
 * every run - so every capture containing one stops being comparable, which
 * is the property `capture-screens.sh`'s whole header is about protecting.
 *
 * SO THE RINGS ARE STILL. `HeroSlot` made that call in Task 13 (its own note:
 * "the rotation is not reproduced here and is not missed on a still
 * surface") and `HeroBand` makes it again at 264px in Task 14c. The
 * difference between a decision and a drop is that this is written down, and
 * a reviewer who disagrees now has the mechanism and the two objections in
 * one place to disagree with.
 * ================================================================
 */
.option-row {
    transition-property: border-color, background-color;
    transition-duration: var(--motion-quick);
}

.t-num {
    transition-property: color;
    transition-duration: var(--motion-quick);
}

.reveal-flare {
    transition-property: opacity, scale;
    transition-duration: var(--motion-screen);
    transition-timing-function: ease-out;
}
.reveal-flare--hidden { opacity: 0; scale: 0.9 0.9; }
